GRAVITATIONAL REDSHIFT
→ 530.11 → Relativity

A photon climbing out of a gravitational well arrives with less energy than it started with.

Spacetime curvature that slows proper time near a mass also stretches every electromagnetic wave that escapes.

Crests emitted a proper interval apart are received far away after a longer coordinate time, so frequency falls and wavelength grows.

FREQUENCY RATIO (Schwarzschild)

    ν_∞ / ν  =  √(1 − 2GM / r c²)

WEAK-FIELD LIMIT

    Δν / ν  =  ΔΦ / c²

VARIABLES
  ν      frequency at emission (proper time)
  ν_∞    frequency measured at infinity
  G      Newton's gravitational constant
  M      mass of the source
  r      radial coordinate of emission
  c      speed of light
  Φ      Newtonian gravitational potential
  Δν     observed frequency shift
  ΔΦ     difference in gravitational potential

FUNDAMENTALS
  • Gravitational time dilation: clocks run slower deeper in a potential well.
  • Photons lose energy (redshift) climbing out; they gain energy (blueshift) falling in.
  • Einstein derived the effect from the equivalence principle in 1907.
  • Pound–Rebka confirmed it with gamma rays in 1959.
